---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Donor CRM software for nonprofits"
  description="Bloomerang, Little Green Light, DonorDock, Neon and more — donor CRMs compared by real price, database size, and the shop they actually fit."
  section="donor-crm">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Donor CRM' }]} />
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">Category guide</p>
      <h1>Donor CRM software</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The donor CRM is your database of record — every donor, gift, and
        relationship in one place. Credible options start around $45/mo (Little
        Green Light) and climb past $100/mo (Bloomerang, Neon, DonorDock) as
        databases and feature needs grow. It's the most-shopped category in
        nonprofit software, and the one people most often buy backwards.
      </p>

      <h2>How pricing actually works</h2>
      <p>
        Almost every donor CRM prices by the size of your database, not your
        team: <strong>Little Green Light</strong> from $45/mo up to 2,500
        constituents, <strong>Bloomerang</strong> from $79/mo for roughly 1,000
        records, <strong>DonorSnap</strong> from $39/mo,
        <strong>Neon CRM</strong> from about $99/mo scaling with organizational
        revenue, <strong>DonorDock</strong> from around $98/mo with unlimited
        contacts. The lesson hiding in that model: clean your list before you
        migrate. You pay every month to store lapsed records you'll never
        re-engage.
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        Pick by who will run it. A solo fundraiser needs approachable and cheap —
        Little Green Light for value, DonorDock for the guided to-do-list feel. A
        team of three to eight needs retention analytics and reporting the whole
        staff can use — that's Bloomerang's home turf. Membership and event-heavy
        organizations should look at Neon's breadth before buying separate tools.
        And the perennial advice from people who've done migrations: the CRM you
        actually keep updated beats the more powerful one you don't.
      </p>

      <h2>The one mistake to avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Don't start your stack here. The CRM is the last decision, not the first
        — a grassroots shop can run on free tools for years, and the research and
        processing layers usually return more per dollar. When you do buy, plan
        the migration seriously: gift history, soft credits, and household
        records are where data dies in transit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="donor-crm" />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The first real CRM enters at <a href="/stacks/250k-1m/">$250k–$1M</a>;
        see how it upgrades at <a href="/stacks/1m-5m/">$1M–$5M</a> and
        <a href="/stacks/5m-plus/">$5M+</a>.
      </p>
      <AffiliateDisclosure />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
